WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.118
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online IELTS preparation.

2
00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:08.828
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.128 --> 00:00:16.424
Unit two: Listening: Form Completion. Today you will complete forms in Section 1 with the exact words you hear.

4
00:00:16.724 --> 00:00:19.632
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:23.741
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.583 --> 00:00:25.225
Form.

7
00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:29.029
Form means a paper with spaces for information.

8
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:30.745
Fill in the form.

9
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.608
Your turn. Say: Form.

10
00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:37.294
Form.

11
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:39.051
Word limit.

12
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:43.036
Word limit means the maximum words you may write.

13
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.499
The word limit is two words.

14
00:00:46.083 --> 00:00:48.666
Your turn. Say: Word limit.

15
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.698
Word limit.

16
00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:54.451
Spell out.

17
00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:58.193
Spell out means to say each letter of a word.

18
00:00:58.783 --> 00:01:00.365
Please spell out your surname.

19
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.684
Your turn. Say: Spell out.

20
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:07.697
Spell out.

21
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:09.439
Postcode.

22
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.378
Postcode means letters and numbers for an address area.

23
00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:16.308
The postcode is CB2 1TN.

24
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:19.650
Your turn. Say: Postcode.

25
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.478
Postcode.

26
00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:24.939
Booking.

27
00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.391
Booking means a reservation made in advance.

28
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:30.693
I made a booking for Friday.

29
00:01:31.283 --> 00:01:33.831
Your turn. Say: Booking.

30
00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:37.552
Booking.

31
00:01:38.317 --> 00:01:39.504
Enquiry.

32
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:43.608
Enquiry means a question asking for information.

33
00:01:44.217 --> 00:01:46.530
She made an enquiry about prices.

34
00:01:47.117 --> 00:01:50.106
Your turn. Say: Enquiry.

35
00:01:52.967 --> 00:01:54.165
Enquiry.

36
00:01:54.917 --> 00:01:55.953
Predict.

37
00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.518
Predict means to guess what answer type is needed.

38
00:02:00.117 --> 00:02:01.896
Predict a name or a number.

39
00:02:02.483 --> 00:02:04.880
Your turn. Say: Predict.

40
00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:08.502
Predict.

41
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.205
Correction.

42
00:02:11.217 --> 00:02:14.110
Correction means a changed answer the speaker gives.

43
00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:16.240
Listen for the correction.

44
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.433
Your turn. Say: Correction.

45
00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:23.361
Correction.

46
00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:27.945
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:30.945
Visal, what happens in Section 1?

48
00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:40.612
Two people have an everyday conversation, such as a booking or an enquiry. You complete a form with names, numbers, dates and addresses.

49
00:02:41.717 --> 00:02:43.461
How do I avoid mistakes?

50
00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:53.890
Read the form first and predict the answer type. Then check the word limit, because writing three words when the limit is two words is marked wrong.

51
00:02:54.983 --> 00:02:56.344
What about spelling?

52
00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:08.705
Names are often spelt out letter by letter. Write them carefully, and listen for corrections like: Sorry, it is Smyth, not Smith.

53
00:03:09.867 --> 00:03:11.704
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:16.988
Grammar focus: Strategy: predict, listen, write exactly.

55
00:03:17.288 --> 00:03:18.463
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:22.241
Read the form and predict a name, a number or a date.

57
00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.427
If the limit is TWO WORDS, you write no more than two words.

58
00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:36.207
Before the audio starts, read the form, underline the answer type and note the word limit. The first answer you hear may be corrected, so wait for the final one.

59
00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:41.251
Be careful! Don't say: I write three words for a two-word limit.

60
00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.860
Say: I write only two words for a two-word limit.

61
00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.602
Obey the word limit.

62
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.750
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:53.812
A Phone Booking.

64
00:03:54.112 --> 00:03:56.100
A caller phones a language school.

65
00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:59.471
She gives her name and spells it slowly.

66
00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:05.218
First she says the course starts on the fifth, then she corrects it to the fifteenth.

67
00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:09.440
The receptionist asks for her postcode and phone number.

68
00:04:10.433 --> 00:04:13.199
The form needs only two words for the job title.

69
00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:18.255
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:19.117 --> 00:04:21.232
Which should you do first in Section 1?

71
00:04:21.532 --> 00:04:25.836
A, write fast. B, stop listening. C, read the form.

72
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.527
The answer is C: read the form.

73
00:04:32.683 --> 00:04:35.905
The word limit is TWO WORDS. Which answer is correct?

74
00:04:36.205 --> 00:04:41.936
A, student nurse. B, a student nurse. C, nurse student teacher.

75
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.929
The answer is A: student nurse.

76
00:04:49.083 --> 00:04:53.709
The caller says: the fifth, sorry, the fifteenth. The answer is blank.

77
00:04:54.009 --> 00:04:57.396
A, fifty. B, fifth. C, fifteenth.

78
00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:02.643
The answer is C: fifteenth.

79
00:05:03.783 --> 00:05:05.318
Names are usually blank.

80
00:05:05.618 --> 00:05:10.468
A, not spoken. B, written in the question. C, spelt out.

81
00:05:14.033 --> 00:05:16.277
The answer is C: spelt out.

82
00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:19.428
What does the receptionist ask for?

83
00:05:19.728 --> 00:05:23.660
A, postcode. B, passport. C, salary.

84
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.420
The answer is A: postcode.

85
00:05:30.633 --> 00:05:33.669
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:34.517 --> 00:05:37.991
Describe how you prepare before a Listening section starts.

87
00:05:46.783 --> 00:05:48.133
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:48.433 --> 00:06:04.300
Before the audio starts, I read the form and underline the key words. I predict whether each answer is a name, a number or a date, and I check the word limit. While I listen, I wait for any correction before I write the final answer.

89
00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:06.309
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:07.217 --> 00:06:13.456
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: predict, listen, write exactly.

91
00:06:13.756 --> 00:06:19.009
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

92
00:06:19.309 --> 00:06:22.043
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

93
00:06:22.343 --> 00:06:25.019
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

